---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const REPO = 'https://github.com/luciancic/foraging';
---
<Base title="About — Forage MTL" page="about" description="About Forage MTL — an open-source foraging map and plant field guide for Montréal, and the data behind its scouting leads.">
  <section class="wrap section prose">
    <h1 style="margin:0 0 10px;">About</h1>
    <p>
      <strong>Forage MTL</strong> is a personal, hand-curated map of foraging spots and a field guide to
      urban edible plants around the island of Montréal. Confirmed spots are places I've actually visited;
      everything else on the map is a <em>scouting lead</em> — a hint from open data that still needs to be
      checked in person before it's trustworthy.
    </p>

    <h2>Open source</h2>
    <p>
      This whole site — the map, the plant guides, the little live API behind the pins — is open source.
      You're welcome to read the code, learn from it, file an issue, or fork it for your own city.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={REPO} target="_blank" rel="noopener">View the source on GitHub →</a>
    </p>

    <h2 id="data">Scouting-leads data sources</h2>
    <p>
      The unverified leads on the map (the circles, diamonds, and triangles) come from three open datasets.
      They're a one-time snapshot, not a live feed, and their locations can be imprecise or out of date —
      which is exactly why they're marked as leads and must be confirmed on the ground before they become
      real spots. Credit and licences:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>iNaturalist</strong> (● circles) — research-grade community observations
        from <a href="https://www.inaturalist.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">iNaturalist</a>. A sighting
        of a species isn't the same as permission or a good picking spot — treat each as a hint.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Falling Fruit</strong> (◆ diamonds) — community-mapped edible spots
        from <a href="https://fallingfruit.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Falling Fruit</a>,
        used under <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a> (attribution,
        non-commercial, share-alike).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ville de Montréal</strong> (▲ triangles) — a sample of the city's public street- and park-tree
        inventory from <a href="https://donnees.montreal.ca/dataset/arbres" target="_blank" rel="noopener">données Montréal</a>,
        used under <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY 4.0</a>. The
        city notes these locations may be imprecise or outdated.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Forage responsibly</h2>
    <p>
      Never eat anything you can't identify with certainty against a live plant and two independent sources.
      Learn the poisonous lookalike before the edible. Forage lightly, respect private property and city
      bylaws, and leave plenty behind. When in doubt, throw it out.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
